     1	# Unattended growth: CSDP steps 3 to 7 without a user, one document at a time
     2	
     3	Load this when a host pipeline grows one model over many documents with no person to
     4	answer ([SKILL.md](../SKILL.md), "Unattended mode"). The skill's Steps 3 to 7 are written
     5	as questions to a user. This file says what an unattended host does at each of them. The
     6	rules the validator enforces (`semantic:unattended-u1` to `-u8`, span units, refusals) stay
     7	in [representation-format.md](../../gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#unattended-source);
     8	nothing here relaxes them.
     9	
    10	A host that stops after Steps 1 and 2 produces a model of elementary facts and nothing
    11	else: every value type a `String`, no subtypes, no uniqueness or mandatory rule, and entity
    12	types that are kinds of one another modelled apart, so a fact about an insurer never joins
    13	with a fact about a party. The rest of this file is what prevents that.
    14	
    15	## Ground rules
    16	
    17	- **Evidence licenses a proposal; it never confirms one.** Every constraint is `proposed` and
    18	  `deontic`. No `explicit` or `user-supplied` source (U3), no `alethic` for `sample`,
    19	  `llm-inferred` or `common-sense` (U8). Nobody reviewed anything.
    20	- **Every proposal cites a span.** The reply carries a verbatim `quote`; the host locates it
    21	  and computes the span (see [the reply shape](#the-reply-shape)). A quote that is absent or
    22	  ambiguous drops the proposal; offsets are never guessed.
    23	- **A cue is a reason to propose, not proof.** Read the sentence. "The insurer must notify the
    24	  broker within 30 days" is a duty a party may breach, and a recorded breach is still a valid
    25	  fact. It is not a rule that every insurer has a notification fact. Say so in `rationale`
    26	  or leave the proposal out, and put the doubt in `open_questions`.
    27	- **Read the model before proposing.** Reuse existing object type and fact type ids. A new
    28	  element that restates an existing one is the failure this file exists to prevent.
    29	- **Never emit an inferred operation.** Operations are commitments about side effects; an
    30	  unattended host does not guess them.
    31	
    32	## What one round is
    33	
    34	One round is one document, in four moves.
    35	
    36	1. **Steps 1 and 2** for the new document: elementary facts, object types, fact types, sample
    37	   rows, each citing a span.
    38	2. **Steps 3 to 6 on the round's new types and facts only.** Walk the evidence tables below
    39	   for the fact types and object types this round introduces. Do not revisit earlier rounds
    40	   here; that is the consolidation round.
    41	3. **Emit the reply** ([shape](#the-reply-shape)), and have the host write the candidate model
    42	   and a proposal. When the round's document is new to the model, the proposal opens with one
    43	   `source-document` `add` (the SHA-256 of the document's raw bytes and a label), before the
    44	   elements whose spans cite it. Declare each document once: a document already in
    45	   `source.documents` is not declared again, and a digest declared twice makes a later change
    46	   to it ambiguous. The operation's rules are in the
    47	   [evolution contract](../../gnd-model-evolve/references/evolution-contract.md#stable-locators). (Before the operation existed the host edited
    48	   `source.documents` by hand before the round; a declaration that appears in the candidate
    49	   with no operation is still refused.)
    50	4. **Gate.** `gnd validate MODEL --document DOC ... --require-documents`, supplying every declared
    51	   document, so a span left unchecked fails the round instead of passing silently (the counts
    52	   are in [representation-format.md](../../gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#unattended-source)).
    53	   Then `gnd evolve preview --policy additive --shape-report`, adding `--check-population FILE`
    54	   when the round adds a constraint on a fact type that already holds facts
    55	   ([below](#constraints-on-fact-types-that-already-hold-facts)), then `apply` repeating
    56	   `--policy additive` and any `--populated` and `--check-population` options. `--shape-report`
    57	   is a preview option only; `apply` rejects it (exit 64). The shape report is advice: for each added fact type it lists existing fact types
    58	   over the same role players. Deciding that two readings mean the same thing is the host's
    59	   ([acting on a shape match](#acting-on-a-shape-match)).
    60	
    61	### Acting on a shape match
    62	
    63	For each existing fact type the report names beside an added one, the host does one of three
    64	things and logs which:
    65	
    66	- **Reuse the existing id.** The two readings say the same thing: drop the added fact type and
    67	  write its facts against the existing one.
    68	- **Keep both, with the reason.** The readings differ ("is returned via" is not "arrives via"):
    69	  say why in the added fact type's `provenance.rationale`.
    70	- **Keep both, and record the doubt** in `open_questions`, for the consolidation round to
    71	  settle.
    72	
    73	Never merge, rename or delete in the round. A merge is a rename or delete of something that
    74	exists, so it belongs to the consolidation round's reviewed proposal.
    75	
    76	**Constraints travel with their fact type.** Under `--policy additive` a new constraint over a
    77	fact type that already holds facts is refused (`constraint.populated.add`, exit 3) unless a
    78	clean population clears it ([below](#constraints-on-fact-types-that-already-hold-facts)), and a
    79	fact type the same proposal adds is never populated. So propose a fact type's uniqueness, mandatory,
    80	value and ring constraints in the round that adds it, before its facts are extracted. A
    81	constraint that spans fact types, or a `value` constraint on an object type, is populated if
    82	**any** fact type it touches is, so without that population it is admitted only when every one
    83	of them is new or empty.
    84	A population held outside the model (extracted facts in a separate file) is invisible to the
    85	policy unless the host passes `--populated FACT_TYPE` for it; leave that out and the policy
    86	admits constraints it should have refused. The policy protects the facts it can see, and only
    87	those. The cells are in the
    88	[evolution contract](../../gnd-model-evolve/references/evolution-contract.md#additive-policy).
    89	
    90	## Starting from a reviewed vocabulary
    91	
    92	A growth run can start from a vocabulary an owner has already reviewed, without restating its claims
    93	as unreviewed. Put the reviewed model in its own file (version 1 to 4, not unattended) and make the
    94	growth root a version-5 model with `source.mode: unattended` and `reviewer: none` that includes it by
    95	id, path and SHA-256. The root needs no document of its own while it holds nothing but the include;
    96	the first round declares one with a `source-document` add.
    97	
    98	What the rules ask of each side is stated once, in
    99	[Unattended source](../../gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#an-unattended-root-that-includes-a-reviewed-model):
   100	the included elements keep their own source, status and modality; everything a round adds is the
   101	root's and keeps every rule. A round may add object types, fact types and constraints, and may attach
   102	constraints to an included fact type (`fact_type_constraints`); it cannot change, rename or delete an
   103	included element, and it cannot re-pin an include. `gnd validate` prints one `INCLUDE` line per
   104	include, naming what it carries. The new constraint's source follows
   105	[Evolve admission (D-12)](../../gnd-orm-core/references/provenance-and-modality.md#evolve-admission-d-12).
   106	
   107	The packaged example `src/gnd/examples/seeded_growth/` runs the whole path: a seed, an empty root,
   108	two rounds, then `gnd validate`, `gnd check` and `gnd judgments check`.
   109	
   110	When the vocabulary cannot be included (it is unattended itself, or nests includes), carry its claims
   111	into the root as `imported`, `proposed` and `deontic`, with the vocabulary file as the root's first
   112	document and a span on each element. Inside the growth model those claims then read as unreviewed.
   113	
   114	## Evidence for Steps 3 to 7
   115	
   116	For each step: the text that licenses a proposal, what to propose, and what does not license
   117	one. `basis` is how the host sets `source`: `text-cue` is `llm-inferred` with
   118	`rationale_world_fact` restating the cue, `rows` is `sample`, `library` is `common-sense`
   119	citing `library_entry`. Library-basis constraints need no span (U5) but may carry one.
   120	
   121	### Step 3: combine entity types; derived or constrained
   122	
   123	| Evidence | Propose | Not evidence |
   124	|---|---|---|
   125	| A definition equating two names: "the Insured (the Policyholder)", "X means Y", "also called" | Do not create two types. Use one id and put the other name in `description`. | Two names that merely co-occur in a sentence |
   126	| Two types already in the model that the no-overlap test (below) flags | A consolidation proposal ([below](#the-consolidation-round)), never an in-round merge | |
   127	| A defined term computed from other facts: "an eligible policy is one that is in force and unclaimed" | A structured derived fact type, only if the rule grammar in `representation-format.md` can express it | Arithmetic or aggregation: record it in `open_questions`; do not write an opaque rule |
   128	
   129	### Step 4: uniqueness and arity
   130	
   131	| Cue in the text | Proposal | Basis |
   132	|---|---|---|
   133	| "the sole", "exactly one", "a single", "only one", "at most one" | `uniqueness` on the role of the thing that has one | `text-cue` |
   134	| "each X has one Y" with sample rows showing no repeats | same, if the text states it; if only the rows show it | `text-cue`, else `rows` |
   135	| Plural or open phrasing: "one or more", "any number of", "each of the", "may have several" | none; this is evidence against | |
   136	
   137	Choose the constrained role from the sentence: in "each policy has exactly one expiry date" the
   138	unique role is the policy's. Then run the arity checks below on any fact type with three or
   139	more roles.
   140	
   141	### Step 5: mandatory roles
   142	
   143	| Cue | Proposal | Basis |
   144	|---|---|---|
   145	| "each X must have Y", "every X has Y", "X shall be", "exactly one" (also unique) | `mandatory` on X's role | `text-cue` |
   146	| "may", "if any", "where applicable", "optionally", "at its option" | none | |
   147	| A duty of a party rather than a property of the record | see [ground rules](#ground-rules) | |
   148	
   149	### Step 6: value, set-comparison, ring and subtyping
   150	
   151	**6a. Typed primitives.** Declare a value type with the narrowest primitive the text supports,
   152	not `String`: an ISO date (`2025-12-31`) is `Date` (sample values must be `YYYY-MM-DD`); a
   153	date and time is `DateTime`; a length of time in days, months or years is `Duration` (the
   154	calendar-day fragment, model version 3: [calendar-day terms](../../gnd-orm-core/references/representation-format.md#calendar-day-terms-v71));
   155	an amount of money or a percentage is `Number` or `Float`. A text that gives a date in prose
   156	("1 March 2024") the host cannot normalise reliably stays `String` with the doubt in
   157	`open_questions`. Then name the value type so the typed library fires: `Money` or `Amount`,
   158	`Percentage`, `Duration`, `Date` and the rest match by name, and each hit is a
   159	`common-sense` proposal ([constraint-inference.md](../../gnd-orm-core/references/constraint-inference.md)).
   160	A `String` value type that already exists is not retyped in a round: changing a primitive is a
   161	non-additive edit, so queue it for the consolidation round.
   162	
   163	**6b. Ring patterns** for a binary fact type whose two roles have the same player: the ring
   164	library, basis `library`.
   165	
   166	**6c. Value constraints.**
   167	
   168	| Cue | Proposal |
   169	|---|---|
   170	| A closed list: "one of", "either A or B", "means (a), (b) or (c)", "only the following", "is limited to" | `value` with `allowed`, on the value type, basis `text-cue` |
   171	| An open list: "such as", "including", "e.g.", "for example", "and the like" | none |
   172	| A numeric bound stated in the text: "not less than 10", "up to 30 days" | `value` with `range`, `text-cue` |
   173	
   174	Propose the enumeration in the round that introduces the value type; against an existing
   175	populated one it is refused (the constraint ranges over every fact type the value type plays in).
   176	
   177	**Enumeration or subtype.** A closed list of categories that carry no roles of their own is an
   178	enumeration (`value` with `allowed`). A category whose instances play roles the others do not
   179	is a subtype. When in doubt, take the enumeration: it can be promoted to subtypes in a
   180	consolidation round, and a wrong subtype cannot be taken back additively.
   181	
   182	**6d. Subtypes.** The text licenses a subtype link when a defined term names a kind of another:
   183	"X, as the insurer", "an Insurer is a Party that…", "the insuring party (the Insurer)". Apply the
   184	role-based test in [csdp-workflow.md](csdp-workflow.md) ("Subtype check"): does the subtype
   185	play a role the supertype does not? If not, it is a role or a value of the supertype. A link is
   186	recorded as `subtype_of` and `inherits_reference` on the subtype's object type, with the
   187	defining sentence appended to that object type's `provenance.spans` and the doubt in
   188	`provenance.rationale`. **A subtype link is structural: it has no `status` or `modality`, so it
   189	cannot be kept `proposed` the way a constraint is.** Its only controls are the span, the
   190	`rationale`, and the fact that a link over existing entity types is applied through the
   191	consolidation round, where an owner sees a preview. In a round, link only a subtype that is new
   192	in the same round.
   193	
   194	**Subtype completeness.** Declare it with a `subtype-partition` constraint only when the text
   195	states it: "exactly one of" or "is either" gives `exclusive` and `exhaustive`; "may also be"
   196	gives neither and no constraint; "or other kinds" says not exhaustive. A partition with neither
   197	flag asserts nothing and fails validation. Because the constraint ranges over the supertype and
   198	every subtype, it is refused additively when any of them already plays a role in a populated
   199	fact type.
   200	
   201	### Step 7: other constraints, modality, commit, validate
   202	
   203	| Cue | Constraint family |
   204	|---|---|
   205	| "only if", "unless", "before", "no later than", a stated order of two dates | `value-comparison`, or a `subset` between two roles when one fact presupposes another |
   206	| "at least two", "no more than three" per entity | `frequency` |
   207	| "a P is never also a Q" | `exclusion` |
   208	
   209	All `proposed` and `deontic`; Step 7a to 7e collapse to nothing (nobody to review). Step 7f runs
   210	as written, with `gnd validate --document` as the gate. Rejected proposals go to
   211	`rejected_proposals` so the next round does not re-propose them.
   212	
   213	## The consolidation round
   214	
   215	Per-round growth cannot see across documents, so a model grown by rounds alone drifts toward
   216	many types that are kinds of one another. Run a consolidation round over the **whole** model
   217	periodically. Every ten documents is a reasonable default, and sooner when the shape report
   218	flags several fact types over the same role players or an entity type count outgrows the
   219	fact type count. The cadence is the host's.
   220	
   221	A consolidation round is Step 3's combining of entity types and Step 6's subtypes, run over
   222	every object type and fact type, using the checks below. It produces a **proposal for review,
   223	not an auto-applied change**:
   224	
   225	- **`--policy additive` does not admit it.** A new supertype alone is an added object type and
   226	  is admitted, but it adds nothing until the existing entity types point at it, and adding
   227	  `subtype_of` or `inherits_reference` to an existing object type is an `object_types.modify`
   228	  outside the admitted keys (`description`, `provenance.rationale`). It is refused whether or
   229	  not the fact types are populated. Nothing existing is renamed, so "adds meaning without
   230	  renaming anything" is true of the result and not of what the policy can admit.
   231	- **Run the consolidation preview without `--policy additive`.** Adding subtype links is a
   232	  `modify`, which needs no breaking acceptance but carries a digest-bound blast radius.
   233	  Merging two names into one is a rename or delete, which is breaking: the owner must accept the
   234	  exact report digest ([gnd-model-evolve](../../gnd-model-evolve/SKILL.md)). A host stops at the
   235	  written preview and hands it over, unless the owner has delegated that apply in terms.
   236	- A subtype partition over populated fact types is a non-additive constraint too; propose it in
   237	  the same consolidation proposal.
   238	
   239	## Checks for a model built from many documents
   240	
   241	Adapted from RelationalAI's `rai-ontology-design` skill (rai-agent-skills). Apply them in the
   242	round to new types and in the consolidation round to all of them. Each ends in a proposal or
   243	an `open_questions` entry, never in a silent edit.
   244	
   245	| Check | Test | If it fails |
   246	|---|---|---|
   247	| **No overlap** | Two object types whose instances are always the same set: every A is a B and every B is an A (same reference, same roles in the same kinds of facts) | They are one type. Consolidate. Two names for one entity set is not two types |
   248	| **Not a property group** | An entity type that only bundles values of another, has no identity of its own and is referenced by nothing else | Make its values roles of the parent. Exception: it is shared by several parents or has its own lifecycle (an address two customers share) |
   249	| **Hidden intermediate type** | Several fact types hang off one entity with the same join to another entity, and each carries a different attribute of that other entity ("policy has broker name", "policy has broker city") | The other entity is missing. Add it and move the attributes to it |
   250	| **Subtype completeness** | A set of subtypes of one supertype | Declare it exclusive, exhaustive or a partition, per the evidence above, or declare none |
   251	| **Subtype or subset constraint** | The filtered set has roles or reference of its own | Subtype. If it is only a condition on the supertype with no roles of its own, use a constraint and no new type. A set reused in three or more places is worth naming |
   252	| **Irreducibility** | A fact type of three or more roles that can be said as two independent facts without losing information ("Supplier supplies Part in Quantity", where supply and quantity do not depend on each other) | Split it |
   253	| **Minimum key span** | In an n-ary fact type, the smallest unique role set spans at least n-1 roles; a single role alone determining the rest means the fact is compound | Split it |
   254	| **Enumeration or subtype** | Closed list of categories | See [6c](#step-6-value-set-comparison-ring-and-subtyping) |
   255	
   256	Irreducibility and minimum key span apply to the round's new fact types of three or more
   257	roles, in the round. The other checks need the whole model and run in the consolidation round.
   258	
   259	## The reply shape
   260	
   261	A host's request must ask for every part below. A reply covering fact types only is the
   262	failure this file exists to prevent. The reply is a proposal the host translates, with no
   263	offsets and no provenance fields of the model's: the host owns the mapping, GND does not
   264	read the reply, and `gnd validate --document` is the only gate.
   265	
   266	A reply carries only what the document adds. An object type or fact type the model already has,
   267	its own or included, is named by its id wherever a field takes an id and is never repeated in
   268	`object_types` or `fact_types`. Restating it costs the tokens of a reply twice over, and a
   269	restated fact type with rows is a change the additive policy refuses. In the example the model
   270	already holds `party` and `policy_has_holder`: the reply names them and repeats neither.
   271	
   272	```json
   273	{
   274	  "document": "policy.txt",
   275	  "object_types": [
   276	    {"id": "policy", "name": "Policy", "kind": "entity",
   277	     "reference": {"mode": "popular", "value_type": "policy_no"}, "quote": "Policy P-17"}
   278	  ],
   279	  "fact_types": [
   280	    {"id": "policy_expires_on_expiry_date", "reading": "{Policy} expires on {ExpiryDate}",
   281	     "roles": [{"object_type": "policy", "role_name": "policy"},
   282	               {"object_type": "expiry_date", "role_name": "expiry_date"}],
   283	     "quote": "Policy P-17 expires on 2025-12-31",
   284	     "rows": [{"values": {"policy": "P-17", "expiry_date": "2025-12-31"},
   285	               "quote": "Policy P-17 expires on 2025-12-31"}]}
   286	  ],
   287	  "subtype_links": [
   288	    {"subtype": "insurer", "supertype": "party", "inherits_reference": true,
   289	     "quote": "An insurer is a kind of party", "rationale": "A defined term naming a kind of party."}
   290	  ],
   291	  "constraints": [
   292	    {"host": "policy_expires_on_expiry_date", "basis": "text-cue",
   293	     "constraint": {"type": "uniqueness", "roles": ["policy"]},
   294	     "cue": "exactly one", "quote": "Each policy has exactly one expiry date",
   295	     "rationale": "The text gives each policy one expiry date."},
   296	    {"host": "policy_has_holder", "basis": "text-cue",
   297	     "constraint": {"type": "mandatory", "role": "policy"},
   298	     "cue": "Every policy has", "quote": "Every policy has a holder",
   299	     "rationale": "The text gives every policy a holder."}
   300	  ],
   301	  "derived_fact_types": [
   302	    {"id": "policy_is_dated", "reading": "{Policy} is dated",
   303	     "roles": [{"object_type": "policy", "role_name": "policy"}],
   304	     "derivation": "derived", "storage": "computed",
   305	     "derivation_rule": {
   306	       "body": [{"fact_type": "policy_expires_on_expiry_date", "bind": {"policy": "$p", "expiry_date": "$d"}}],
   307	       "head": {"policy": "$p"}},
   308	     "quote": "A dated policy is a policy that has an expiry date",
   309	     "rationale": "A defined term computed from a fact the model already holds."}
   310	  ],
   311	  "open_questions": [
   312	    {"step": 6, "note": "The text gives a limit in prose that was not typed.", "quote": "within the notice period"}
   313	  ]
   314	}
   315	```
   316	
   317	| Field | Rule |
   318	|---|---|
   319	| `quote` | A verbatim excerpt of the document. The host searches the NFC text; no match, or more than one without a `quote_occurrence` (0-based), drops the entry and logs it |
   320	| `object_types`, `fact_types` | New elements only, in the model's own element shapes; `quote` becomes `provenance.spans`. An element the model already has, its own or included, is named by its id where a field takes an id and is not repeated here; the host drops a repeated one and logs it |
   321	| `rows` | Rows for a new fact type only, one entry per sample row; `quote` becomes the row's `sample_spans` entry (U6 needs every row named once). Rows the document gives for a fact type the model already has are an `open_questions` entry: the additive policy refuses a changed sample row (`fact_types.modify` in the evolution contract's additive matrix) |
   322	| `subtype_links` | Sets `subtype_of` and `inherits_reference` on the subtype, appends the span to its `provenance.spans`, puts `rationale` in `provenance.rationale` |
   323	| `constraints` | `host` is a fact type id, or `model` for a top-level constraint. `constraint` is the constraint body as the [constraint reference](../../gnd-orm-core/references/constraint-reference.md) gives it, without `source`, `status`, `modality` or `provenance`. The host writes those: `text-cue` is `llm-inferred` with `rationale_world_fact` set to `cue`, `rows` is `sample`, `library` is `common-sense` with `library_entry`; always `proposed` and `deontic` |
   324	| `derived_fact_types` | The model's fact type shape with `derivation: derived`, `storage: computed` and a structured `derivation_rule`, the object form that `representation-format.md` defines under derived fact types. `quote` is the sentence that defines the term and becomes `provenance.spans`; `rationale` goes to `provenance.rationale`. No `rows`: its facts follow from the rule, so it holds no sample. A rule the grammar cannot express is an `open_questions` entry, never a string rule |
   325	| `open_questions` | Anything the evidence does not settle. Not written to the model; kept in the host's log for the owner |
   326	
   327	## Constraints on fact types that already hold facts
   328	
   329	The procedure above prevents this by proposing constraints with the fact type. For a constraint
   330	the text reveals only later, pass the population to the policy: `gnd evolve preview --policy
   331	additive --check-population FILE`, and `apply` with the same options. The policy admits a new
